module gunbc.plans.interpreter_cut_replacement

import std.dissolution { unbound_dissolution }
import std.types { List }
import gunbc.plan { Plan, PlanRetiresWhen }
import std.markdown { MarkdownBlock }
import gunbc.plans.md_helpers { h2, p, li, ul }

fn interpreter_cut_replacement_body() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    p(text: "**Status:** first commit landed on `integration/interpreter-cut` — `v1_interpreter.rs` (14,910 lines) and `v1_interpreter_dispatch_generated.rs` (749) deleted, with the three producer layers that would have regrown them. Delete-first doctrine (operator ruling 2026-08-15, DESIGN §3 via gunbc#8276): the deletion IS the census, and fix-forward proceeds one operator-gated class at a time. The branch is red on main's terms by construction; the seed crate does not link without an execution path, and that is the finding, not a defect to patch."),

    h2(text: "Why the pre-existing designs did not defer this"),
    p(text: "`gunbc.plans.interpreter_kernel_d` ruled that host-physics inside the interpreter was the **terminal irreducible bootstrap kernel** and that dissolution was explicitly not a file delete. Under the 2026-08-15 ruling that plan is QUARRY: its area map (pure-eval vs host-physics vs CLI render) is valid evidence and was used here, but its sequencing was authored while the interpreter lived and is premise-contaminated. The divergence, stated rather than deferred to: there is no irreducible kernel to preserve, because the host-physics arms are reachable only THROUGH interpretation. Delete the reachability and the arms are unreachable code, not a kernel."),

    h2(text: "The regrowth trap — three layers, all cut in the deleting commit"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**Producer.** `gunbc.v1_interpreter_dispatch_emit` (402 lines) folding the 1,972-line roster `gunbc.v1_interpreter_primitive_surface`. Deleting only the emitted artifact leaves the press running."),
      li(text: "**Regen driver.** `gunbc.generated_artifact` carried `V1InterpreterDispatchGeneratedRsArtifact` as a registry variant with artifact path, commit policy and eq arm; `gunbc.generated_artifact_emit` held the producer import plus the generate and extra-valid dispatch arms. The registry alone would have rebuilt the corpse on the next regen, with no reference to the deleted source."),
      li(text: "**A predicate with exactly one inhabitant.** `v1.05_emit_rust` `is_lib_rs_macro_provider` tested for the generated dispatch module by name, and drove BOTH a `#[macro_use]` rendering special case and an ordering special case in `lib.rs` emission — so the emitter would keep printing a declaration for a module that no longer exists. The role is now uninhabited and deleted outright; `lib.rs` declarations are uniform. Cut in the `.dag` authority and its emitted Rust twin together."),
    ]),

    h2(text: "Census — cargo's refusals at identity grain"),
    p(text: "`cargo check --workspace --all-targets`, exit 101, 105 errors, single root `crate::v1_interpreter` absent: E0433 ×44, E0432 ×28, E0282 ×32 (inference cascade off the unresolved imports — not an independent class), E0277 ×1. The lib crate fails first, so bins and tests were never compiled: this is wave one, and a second wave follows once the lib links. **UNBANKED, 2026-08-16 — 105 IS AN ABORT POINT AND NOT A POPULATION, AND THE SAME GOES FOR EVERY LATER COUNT ON THIS BRANCH.** E0432/E0433 are RESOLUTION failures: rustc aborts the target before type checking, so what is reported is where the reporter stopped, not how much is wrong. A later run on this branch reports NINE, and the difference between 105 and 9 measures the abort point moving, not defects being fixed — reading it as progress reads the reporter rather than the tree. The depth is not merely unknown, it is unknowable from here: `cli_run.rs` survives with 462 references into the deleted module, so any count taken while those dangle is a lower bound of unbounded depth, and the true residue is not measurable until the engine cut lands. **The numbers stay on the record as what they are — the observed abort points of a half-cut tree — and no arithmetic between them is a finding.**"),
    p(text: "**The load-bearing finding.** Outside `cli_run` there is ONE class, not several. All seven non-`cli_run` consumers — `coproduct_reflection`, `compiler_tests`, `data_initializer_identity`, `recorded_fixture`, `ci_control_plane`, `pre_push`, `census_exclude_derive` — need the identical quartet: `Value`, `InterpContext`, `ExecutionMode`, and a run entry. Nothing outside `cli_run` reaches the builtin dispatch at all. Corpus reference weight: `Value` 256, `InterpContext` 127, `ExecutionMode` 73, `run_in_context_with_args` 54, `run_in_context` 37, `run` 4."),

    h2(text: "Class one — the eval-entry quartet (WITH THE OPERATOR, analysis only)"),
    p(text: "The class is *call a `.dag` function from Rust and receive a dynamic `Value`*. Its native answer is the emitted-and-compiled path calling a real typed function: the dynamic `Value` universal is the interpreter's own artifact — the single dynamic type every modeled type collapses into so one evaluator can walk them all — and it does not survive into native execution, where each modeled type is its own Rust type. No mini-interpreter, no wholesale restoration. `coproduct_reflection` and `recorded_fixture` may genuinely need name-indexed dispatch; its native form is generated typed wrappers, still not an interpreter. No building before the operator's go."),

    h2(text: "Retirement receipts — granted 2026-08-15"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**Eval profiling — RETIRED, need does not survive.** Its subject was the interpreter's own evaluation; the subject is deleted, so this is a retired need rather than a port. Native profiling, if ever wanted, is a NEW need designed natively. Identity grain: `PerformanceReceipt`, `performance_receipt_from_witness`, `EvalRecomputeTotals`, `take_process_eval_recompute_totals`, `refresh_eval_recompute_trace_enabled_cache_for_tests`, `print_eval_recompute_trace`, `eval_profile_reset`, `eval_call_memo_counters`, `eval_call_memo_frame_exit`, `eval_subject_set`, `eval_subject_clear`, `thread_cpu_nanos`, `WITNESS_COST_CLOCK_BASIS_NOTE`, `map_budget_error_to_witness_refusal`, `untraced_expectation_frontier`, and the snapshot readers `flatten_counters_snapshot`, `flatten_by_site_snapshot`, `dag_fn_self_time_snapshot`, `call_frequency_snapshot`, `builtin_time_snapshot`, `big_fold_by_dag_site_snapshot`, `fold_caller_snapshot`, `list_cons_tail_split_snapshot`, `parse_memo_global_snapshot`."),
      li(text: "**CLI output policy — MISPLACED, not dying; interpreter-side residence deleted, nothing rebuilt.** Terminal rendering was parked inside the interpreter and is not interpretation. A modeled authority already exists at `gunbc.output_policy`, so the native answer DERIVES from that model rather than porting the Rust. Assigned to the `cli_run` cut (tidy-gull-813) as a fix-forward class. Identity grain: `Verbosity`, `cli_verbosity`, `OutputDecision`, `output_decision`, `set_output_policy`, `group_begin`, `group_end`, `set_group_syntax`, `InstalledGroupSyntax`, `host_trace_grouping_active`, `StreamDisposition`, `InstalledEffectStreamPolicy`, `set_effect_stream_policy`, `render_shell_effect_failed_line_mirror`."),
      li(text: "**`bounded_shell_host_drain.rs` — DELETED WITH RECEIPT.** Genuine host physics (bounded child-stderr drain), but its only declaration was a `#[path]` module inside the deleted interpreter, and retention without a consumer is speculative. **Restoration trigger:** the native shell-effect realization — `gunbc.host_effect_realize` `host_effect_apply`'s shell transport — when it needs a bounded stderr drain. Quarry keeps the implementation and its test `bounded_shell_stream_capture_replay.rs` at the pre-cut history."),
    ]),

    h2(text: "Banked obligations from the deleted compiler-test kernel"),
    p(text: "Banked BEFORE deletion, because the inventory is the one thing the delete destroys. Three artifacts died as one cut: `compiler_tests.rs` (3,141 lines, 46 test fns), its producer `v1.compiler.compiler_tests_rust` (`src/v1/compiler_tests_rust.dag`), and that producer's emitted twin `v1_compiler_compiler_tests_rust.rs`. They are one subject, not three: 34 of 41 emitted titles matched titles in `compiler_tests.rs`, so the hand file was largely the producer's OUTPUT. The producer emitted Rust test source text containing `crate::v1_interpreter::run` calls, which makes it the third regrowth-trap instance — regen would have printed calls to a deleted module. The `emit_compiler_tests_module` arm and the `has_compiler_tests` lib.rs parameter (which re-emitted `#[cfg(test)] mod compiler_tests;`) were cut with it, in the `.dag` authority and the emitted twin together. **49 distinct obligations**, proposed split below; obligations earn re-establishment rather than being ported wholesale."),
    p(text: "**Proposed RE-ESTABLISH as normal `.dag` witnesses (26)** — these prove language rules that outlive the seed, and DESIGN §4b names several as the ordinary compiler floor. Call-shape bijection: `call_shape_wall_witness`, `call_shape_duplicate_wall_witness`, `call_deficit_red_witness`. Construction walls: the six `constructor_call_admission_*` rows, `sole_constructor_fieldless_newtype_witness`, `sole_constructor_violation_outside_module`. Type and method floor: `method_existence_wall_witness`, `declared_type_conformance_witness`, `diagnostics_carrier_grounds_to_native_option`, `function_value_named_application_controls_witness`. Coercion (DESIGN §4's one procedure read in three directions): the seven `coercion_*` rows. Grounding and emission: `groupcompletion_int_checkpoint_fires_under_faithful_corpus`, `rust_btree_set_ord_eligibility_requires_nominal_carrier_shape`, `render_rust_applied_type_routes_qualified_base_through_leaf_name`, `unlisted_import_use_witness`, `caret_parse_smoke_native_compile_emit_witnesses`."),
    p(text: "**Proposed RETIRE with receipt (23)** — subject is the v1 seed's own self-compilation or harness plumbing, which is precisely what is being deleted. Self-host harness: `self_compile_all_modules`, `self_compile_cargo_check`, `self_compile_sources`, `self_parse_all_modules`, `self_parse_tokenize_dag`, `self_resolve_all_modules`. Frontend smoke over the v1 pipeline: `parse_trivial_module`, `pipeline_trivial_module`, `tokenize_produces_tokens`, `tokenize_fn_keyword`, `tokenize_ends_with_eof`, `tokenize_count_stable`. Profiling the v1 pipeline — same class as the retired eval profiling, and retired for the same reason: `profile_full_pipeline`, `profile_reconcile_per_module`, `profile_self_compile`. Harness utilities and seed plumbing: `get_rss_bytes`, `type_size_regression_check`, `workspace_root`, `pipeline_occurrence_sidecar_serde_round_trip`, `contracts_sidecar_wired_into_emit_scope`, `function_value_field_method_known_hole_probe`. The split is a PROPOSAL at the boundary, not a decision taken."),

    h2(text: "The rustc boundary — what the seed emitter can actually deliver"),
    p(text: "The replacement for the deleted interpreter is emit-on-demand native execution, per `v2.std.materialize` `materialize_module_note`: run = realize ∘ materialize ∘ dependency_view, with runtime Share realized by native artifacts keyed by content hash and explicitly NOT by parallelizing an interpreter. So the first question is not how many witnesses run natively — it is whether ONE crosses the boundary. Measured on `dag/test/claim/cron_tag_witness_test.dag`, the smallest witness that actually evaluates something (the 6-line `ci_exclusion_proof_test.dag` was rejected as a deliberate `false` sentinel that would prove nothing). **It emits clean and does not compile:** emission exit 0, 19-source closure, 24 files, 8,460 lines, ZERO blocking diagnostics; `cargo build --release` then fails with 102 errors."),
    p(text: "**Four families, one dominant.** (A) 78 errors — 76% — are ONE defect: the emitter emits `.clone()` on values of a generic type parameter and never emits the `T: Clone` bound (E0599 ×54 + E0277 ×24; params T 41, U 3, A 2, B 2). (B) 2 errors are the same defect for a different trait — `==` on `Rc<Vector<T>>` without `PartialEq`. A and B are one story: bounds are dropped when generics are emitted. (C) 4 errors are emission completeness — emitted `use` statements naming modules outside the emitted closure (`std_nat`, `extdeps_cron::cron`, `NamedEdgeTargetLookup`). (D) 14 rendering mismatches (ContentHash carrier 7, `Box<dyn Error>` vs `String` 4, others 3). Profiles are SUBJECT-SENSITIVE and no single number is 'the ceiling': the same emitter on `05_eval` gives ~800 errors dominated by E0308, where this witness is dominated by the missing `Clone` bound."),

    h2(text: "The self-shadowing alias defect — complete census, isolated cause"),
    p(text: "Three E0308s named `std_occurrence_identity::OccurrenceId` against `v2_std_node::OccurrenceId`. **First read — that this was a §3 authority fork in the corpus — was WRONG and is retracted.** `v2.std.node` declares `type OccurrenceId = std.occurrence_identity.OccurrenceId`: an alias, one authority, nothing to repair. The emitter renders it `pub struct OccurrenceId(pub std::marker::PhantomData<()>)` — manufacturing a second nominal Rust type from one modeled type. The error was evidence about the ARTIFACT and was mistaken for evidence about the SOURCE; the general rule is that two type paths in a diagnostic are two Rust types, and concluding two DECLARATIONS requires opening the declarations."),
    p(text: "**The tree holds exactly five cross-module type aliases, so the census is complete rather than sampled.** Emitted: `UInt = v2.std.nat.Nat` → `pub type UInt = Rc<crate::std_nat::Nat>` CORRECT; `Lean4NatValue = v2.std.nat.Nat` → `pub type Lean4NatValue = Rc<Nat>` CORRECT; `List<T> = std.algebra.FreeMonoid<T>` → `pub type List<T> = Vec<T>` CORRECT; `OccurrenceId` → phantom newtype, COLLAPSES. The fifth is a stripped-projection fixture, not emitted. A cross-tree hypothesis is REFUTED by `List<T>`, which is cross-tree and emits perfectly."),
    p(text: "**Cause, isolated by a two-probe minimal pair** (authored in-tree, deleted after): `type ProbeAliasDiffName = std.occurrence_identity.OccurrenceId` emits `pub type ProbeAliasDiffName = OccurrenceId;` while `type OccurrenceId = std.occurrence_identity.OccurrenceId` emits the phantom struct. Same target, same cross-tree reference, same file; the ONE variable is whether the alias's name equals the target's LEAF name. When it does, the emitter resolves the right-hand side's leaf back to the alias itself, finds a declaration that is its own definition, and renders the bodyless case as `PhantomData`. This fits all four emitted rows with zero residue."),
    p(text: "**This is NOT the hollow-alias note's population.** That note's subject is a bodyless declaration reached by construction; this is a well-formed alias the emitter MANUFACTURES a bodyless declaration from, downstream of any corpus lens — so that wall would not catch it, which is a blind side worth knowing. **Ruling: the site is NOT renamed and NOT deleted** (coordinator, 2026-08-15). Renaming would drive the observed frequency to zero while the emitter rule stayed broken — the absorbing fallback executed by an author — and re-exporting a type under its own name is the natural facade shape anyone would write next. `v2.std.node` `OccurrenceId` therefore stands as the discriminating RED for the emitter fix, with the two-probe pair as its positive control. Terminal home for A/B/C and this rule alike is v2's own emitter (`src/v2/compiler/05_emit.dag` `emit` = `serialize_target` ∘ `translate`), never the frozen v1 emitter, whose authority is being deleted and where any ADDITION would die with it."),

    h2(text: "Do the emitter families reproduce in v2? — asked before repairing"),
    p(text: "Families A–D were characterized against the FROZEN v1 emitter, whose authority is being deleted. The terminal home for any emitter rule is v2's own `emit` = `serialize_target` ∘ `translate`, which is measured live: `v2.test.emit.rust_binop_emit` runs interpreted under a main-vintage oracle over a 102-source closure with zero diagnostics, and its two discriminating controls both hold, so emit's output is a real function of its input. But repairing in Y a defect only ever observed in X is how X's decomposition rebuilds itself inside Y, so each family is asked FIRST whether v2 can express its subject at all."),
    p(text: "**A and B — NOT REPRODUCIBLE, because the subject does not exist in v2.** A is `.clone()` on a type-parameter value without a `T: Clone` bound on the signature; B is the same defect for `PartialEq`. Both presuppose a generic function signature. Measured: `type_param` / `TypeParam` / `generic_param` appear ZERO times across `v2.std.node`, `v2.compiler.infer`, `v2.compiler.translate` and `v2.compiler.emit` — the whole node→infer→translate→emit path. The only `type_param` in v2 sits on `TargetCollectionWitnessOrd`/`Hash`/`Eq` in `v2.std.compilers.target_model`, consumed by `v2.compiler.trait_derive_completeness` — the DERIVE-SUPPLEMENTAL obligation, which is a different property that merely shares the word (deriving `Debug` on a generic TYPE requires `Clone` on a slot, versus a FUNCTION BODY cloning a `T`). Corroborated at the property level rather than by name: NO v2 emit test emits a generic function — zero goldens across `src/v2/test/claim/emit` contain a `fn name<…>` shape. So v2 has no signature-with-type-params to omit a bound from. **This is a CAPABILITY GAP in v2's emitter — no generic function support — not a defect and not a rule to port.** Had the v1 fix been carried over, it would have implemented bound emission for a construct v2 cannot build, inventing the feature the bug presupposes in order to host it. Weighting, per operator ruling 2026-08-15: the gap is ORDINARY IMPLEMENTATION WORK — generics took under a few hours in v1 — so nothing here supports reading it as a wall or as a common cause behind any frontier count. What stands is the measurement and the reproduce-in-Y-before-repairing rule; the escalation does not."),
    p(text: "**Disposition for A and B — the v1 repair is RETIRED, the obligation is NOT.** Retiring a patch is not the same as deciding nothing is owed, and stating only *does not reproduce* would let a later reader draw the second from the first. Retired: v1's emitter/ownership realization defect and its fix, whose only subject is a decomposition v2 does not have. **Surviving claim, still enrolled against v2:** generic programs emitted to Rust are well-typed and carry every semantically required constraint. New evidence that discharges it: a v2 generic vertical plus a missing-constraint RED, when generics land there. The 157 generic fn decls and 92 generic types in `src/v2` are that claim's CONSUMER CORPUS AND DENOMINATOR — they are not an implementation sequence and this is not a frontier."),
    p(text: "**Why the v1 repair was never the right artifact, even in v1 — the inference runs the wrong way.** The emitter must never discover that a parameter needs `Clone` by noticing that it happened to emit `.clone()`. That is validation standing exactly where construction belongs (§5): the bound is read back off the emitted text instead of derived from the source's semantics. The two constructions that are right are ownership/lowering making the clone unnecessary, or inference producing an EXPLICIT constraint obligation that the target model carries and the Rust emitter merely serializes. So porting the fix would have imported not just a program v2 cannot build but the wrong DIRECTION of inference. §3 rides along: a target-independent model may not name the concept `RustGeneric` or reduce a constraint to a Rust trait string — `T: Clone` is ONE REALIZATION of a requirement produced upstream."),
    p(text: "**C — SUBJECT IS EXPRESSIBLE; whether the defect reproduces is UNMEASURED.** C is an emitted `use` naming a module outside the emitted closure. Unlike A and B, v2's translate carries real import machinery — `module_import_projection_from_target`, `import_value_from_carrier`, and an `import_node: Node` carrier — so the subject exists and C is a candidate real v2 defect. Expressibility is measured; presence of the defect is NOT, and is not claimed here. **D** is v1 rendering (ContentHash carrier, error types) plus the self-shadowing alias above, and is likewise unasked in v2."),

    h2(text: "DECLARED RUNG LOWERING — two fail-closed guards died with the file"),
    p(text: "Two backstops lived inside `v1_interpreter.rs` and were reached from `eval_binop`'s `Eq`/`Ne` path (`:2913` and `:2916`, with `InterpError::CrossRepresentationEquality` declared `:788` and rendered `:936`): `cross_representation_numeric_straddle` and `cross_family_content_hash_straddle`. **They are NOT at the same rung, and declaring them as one drop was inflation in the honest direction — which §4b forbids exactly as much as the other kind (coordinator ruling, corrected here).** Only one of them is a lowering."),
    p(text: "**The NUMERIC guard is not a rung drop — DESIGN already sanctioned its removal.** Its own words: the guard is *dead-in-corpus for numerics, kept as fail-closed backstop until the `Value::Null` split lands (guard removal bundled with that work, fenced out of this window)*. Dead-in-corpus because #5428 grounded `Nat` construction-side so the native form equals the modeled form. The removal was already scheduled; this cut executes it EARLY, from a different lane. So the receipt is not *a guarantee was lowered* but *a planned removal landed ahead of its bundle* — and that framing carries one obligation the other would have missed: **the `Value::Null` split no longer carries a guard-removal item**, and its plan must say so or a later reader goes looking for a guard that is already gone."),
    p(text: "**The CONTENT-HASH guard IS a real drop, and it is bounded — smaller than this row first stated.** DESIGN scopes Phase A as *carrier construction walls + R0 eval only*: two mechanisms, and this cut takes exactly one. **What survives, structurally:** the carrier walls — `OciDescriptor.digest` → `OciContentDigest`, `GateRosterHash` and `v2.std.node.Hash` → `Fnv1a64Structural`, git trees → `GitObjectId` — and the `HashFamily` grounding itself, which lives in `std_content_hash.rs` and is outside this cut. **Previous rung:** mitigatable — a typed, located runtime refusal at one eval seam, already UNWITNESSED since the #8146 suite cutover deleted its assertions. **Temporary rung:** absent. **Bounded population — the residual, not the class:** bare union `==`/`!=` on `ContentHash` values that already got PAST construction; `Value::eq` stayed infallible as the single `CanonKey` map-key authority and is unaffected. **Reason:** re-adding a guard to a file that must not exist is investment in a dying authority. **Restoration trigger:** Phase B of `feature:content-hash-family-grounded` — substrate typecheck refusing union-level equality — which retires the backstop by making the class UNWRITABLE rather than caught at eval. A climb, not a re-add."),
    p(text: "**And a survivor whose subject retired, found while measuring the above:** `std_content_hash.rs` `content_hash_cross_family_eq_v1_seed_bridge_note` is outside this cut and DESCRIBES the backstop that dies — a string-returning note citing reviews 45323/45331/45353/45359 that, after this commit, asserts a mechanism that is gone. It joins the same census as the scaffold index and the bijection transports."),
    p(text: "**Why this would have shipped silently, which is the transferable half.** Both ends were dark before the cut: the witness was already deleted, and a deleted Rust guard emits no diagnostic about behavior that simply stops happening — the 62 cargo errors are silent about every byte of it. **A deletion census finds what REFERS to you; it cannot find what you were the only one DOING.** It surfaced only by asking, of every dissolution note naming a deleted subject, what that artifact guards NOW — and the note expected to be stale turned out to be the one asserting a live guarantee about the removed file."),
    p(text: "**RULED (coordinator, on the finding): DESIGN.md is now false and this cut owns the correction, in the same PR — through `gunbc.design_document`, never the generated `.md`.** The sentence *the guard itself is untouched and still live in `v1_interpreter.rs` `eval_binop`. What is gone is the proof, not the backstop.* was true when written and this commit falsifies it. That is the §3 stale-citation class landing in the CANONICAL AUTHORITY — the exact failure DESIGN documents against itself twice — so it is corrected at its producer rather than left for a later reader to trip over. The superseded reasoning for deferring it is kept below, because the argument was wrong in a way worth naming: FOUR carriers assert the guard is live, and one of them is the DESIGN producer. `std.content_hash` `content_hash_cross_family_eq_v1_seed_bridge_note` states in terms that the guard is *untouched and still live in `v1_interpreter.rs` `eval_binop`*; `gunbc.plans.model_realization_fork` carries it twice as FAIL-CLOSED and *kept as fail-closed backstop*; `gunbc.design_document` repeats it in the §-open-threads row that generates DESIGN.md. Those sentences were TRUE when written and remain true on main. **The error in deferring was treating that as a reason to wait.** A branch that deletes the mechanism is precisely where the correction belongs — it lands with the deletion, in one commit, so the two never disagree; deferring it would leave the canonical authority asserting a live guarantee for exactly as long as the cut is in flight, which is the interval a reader is most likely to consult it. Load-bearing raises the bar on the edit, it does not license leaving a false claim standing."),
    h2(text: "SURVIVING OBLIGATION — the prepared-scope-index split, retained under a changed terminal contract"),
    p(text: "**Owner UNASSIGNED, and recorded here because this census is the artifact the successor's author will actually read (§3 step 6).** The floor-cut lane holds a patch to `v1_interpreter.rs` that splits `InterpContext` into `PreparedScopeIndexes` — immutable, a pure function of the module population, built once per DISTINCT SCOPE — and the per-claim mutable half. Measured there: **9,573 reconstructions of maps that only 1,155 distinct scopes can possibly differ in**, with a construction counter shipped alongside, because a bound nothing counts is a bound nobody can check. **This cut deleting the file does NOT remove the problem; it relocates it.** Whatever evaluates claims after the deletion meets the same 9,573-vs-1,155 ratio, and it meets it with no label on it."),
    p(text: "**Why it is stated as an obligation rather than ported:** porting the patch forward into a file scheduled for deletion is the investment-in-dying-authority move this cut declines everywhere else, and the patch is load-bearing for its own lane's mechanism today. The transferable content is the shape, not the code — *fresh state per claim is correct; fresh INDEXES per claim is the same defect wearing the word 'fresh'* — which is the entry-major cost shape reproduced one layer BELOW the compiler, after the compiler's own copy of it was removed. A successor that folds one prepared subject over every witness and then rebuilds indexes per claim gives the single-subject saving straight back. **The cross-lane form is the dangerous inverse of the usual merge question:** not *does my replacement re-import a fact your deletion retired*, but *does your deletion retire a fact my replacement depends on* — no conflict, both sides look fine, and the cost reappears later wearing no label."),
    h2(text: "GUARDS THAT LEFT WITH THEIR SUBJECTS — the population no census reaches"),
    p(text: "**A deletion is a census only for consumers OUTSIDE the deletion set.** A consumer inside it cannot refuse, so every obligation it carried is invisible: no diagnostic, no unresolved reference, no red, and it looks exactly like a clean removal. Joining this cut's 24 deleted files against each other for definition-consumed-by-deleted-sibling pairs returns eleven, and the discriminator that matters is **guard or peer** — a peer dying with its subsystem is the cut working; a GUARD dying with its subject is an obligation vanishing, because a guard's whole purpose is to outlive what it constrains."),
    p(text: "**Two guards, recorded as §4b no-silent-regression rows rather than restored.** (1) `v1_interpreter_primitive_surface.dag` was guarded by THREE tests, all deleted in this same cut — the witness test, the roster acceptance test, and the dispatch-authority acceptance test. Among the names they carried is `arm_declares_dissolution_trigger`, so the guard asserting that every dispatch arm declares a dissolution trigger went out WITH the arms it asserted about. (2) `compiler_tests.rs` declared and enforced `SELF_COMPILE_ERROR_RATCHET = 2700` as `error_count <= ratchet`; the symbol exists nowhere in the tree at HEAD. **Not restored, deliberately:** 2700 is a tree-copied measurement pinned as a literal, which DESIGN's own oracle rule rejects — a measurement copied from the same current tree is not an oracle — and its subject population moves under every cut in flight, so restoring it would re-enshrine a number that was never grounded and is now certainly wrong. What is owed is the record that a self-compile error bound existed and no longer does, with its value and assertion shape, which is this row."),
    p(text: "**The class is invisible to the LEDGER as well as to the census, and that is the part worth carrying forward.** Every landing obligation in this program is keyed on artifacts that still exist and need regenerating. A guard that left with its subject produces no artifact, no drift, and no row — so the one instrument built specifically to catch what falls between lanes cannot see this class either. It is reachable only by asking, of a deletion set, what it consumed from itself."),
    h2(text: "GENERATED-ARTIFACT DRIFT — declared exactly, per the fleet ruling"),
    p(text: "**LANDING-OBLIGATION LEDGER — seven artifacts, derived MECHANICALLY rather than from what was interesting enough to write about.** The first version of this section listed one, then five; joining the touched-file set against the registry's own artifact paths produced seven. The two the narrative filter missed are the instructive ones. **(a) `dag/gunbc/stage0_crate_layout_generated.dag`** — a REGISTRY-NAMED artifact hand-edited here (+3 −18) while being filed mentally as a producer; it is both, and it is the INTERMEDIATE in the frontier → layout → `.rs` chain. Its authority was corrected in the same cut (`v2.compiler.self_host.frontier` loses one `SeedRetainedIntrinsicRegistration` row), so the fact has a live home; what cannot be shown is that the bytes are the generator's. **(b) `docs/plans/interpreter-cut-replacement.md` — NOT stale: NEVER GENERATED, and not in git.** Registering this plan in `gunbc.plan_registry_batch_g` minted a committed-artifact obligation at `plan_path` at the moment of registration, outstanding since the first commit of this cut and growing with every receipt added to it. The verb matters: GENERATE, not regenerate."),
    p(text: "**Checked at PRODUCER grain, not per artifact, because per-artifact checking already failed here once:** `v1_compiler_emit_rust.rs` was labelled transitioning on the fact that v1-cut deletes `src/v1/05_emit_rust.dag`, and `lib.rs` — SAME PRODUCER — was left labelled re-derivable in the same breath. Four producers, each diffed against all four sibling branches once: `gunbc.design_document`, `v2.compiler.self_host.frontier` and this plan carrier all survive every planned merge; `src/v1/05_emit_rust.dag` is deleted by v1-cut, and that answer propagates to BOTH artifacts it emits. Blast radius checked rather than assumed — the edit to that producer removes the compiler-tests module arm and the macro-provider classifier, which reach the lib.rs module list and nothing else it emits. **And the ordering constraint is sharper than convenience: no lane deletes `v1_compiler_emit_rust.rs`, only its authority — so the emitted twin is not inert frozen bytes but LIVE frozen bytes, still compiling and still emitting.** Verifying those two against their producer must happen BEFORE v1-cut lands, because afterwards the obligation does not lapse, it converts: the committed bytes become the fact, permanently, unverified."),
    p(text: "**TWO OF THE SEVEN ARE NOT REGENERATIONS AT ALL — they are a self-host fixed point, and a pre-cut binary discharges them WRONG.** `v1_compiler_emit_rust.rs` IS the emitter, and `lib.rs` is emitted BY it; this cut's edits to `src/v1/05_emit_rust.dag` change the emitter's own CODE — a deleted `test_mod` arm, a deleted macro-provider classifier, two collapsed ordering functions — not data the emitter reads. So a main-vintage binary contains the MAIN-VINTAGE emitter, and pointing it at the corrected authority does not produce the corrected bytes: for `lib.rs` it would **re-emit `mod compiler_tests;`**, actively reverting the change, because the old binary has no knowledge of the arm this cut removed. **The discharge is therefore three steps, not one: regenerate, REBUILD THE BINARY FROM THE REGENERATED EMITTER, regenerate again, and require round two byte-identical to round one.** A non-identical second round is not a failed regeneration — it is the observation that the self-host fixed point has not converged on this branch, which is a finding rather than a discharge, and it is exactly what `regen_stage0` exists to detect. The other five rows are ordinary: the old binary reads a new authority and emits an artifact whose own behaviour is not the subject."),
    p(text: "**THE GRAIN RULE RUN OVER THIS CUT'S OWN DELETIONS — per function, not per file, and it found one thing.** The unit that tempts is *it references a deleted symbol*; the correct unit is *is the thing this asserts still true and still needed*, because a compiler error points at a LOCATION and says nothing about whether the CLAIM there still has a subject. Four test carriers were deleted whole here. Three are interpreter-primitive surface, roster and dispatch — subject gone, PEER. The fourth, `test.claim.shell_stream_capture`, is the one whose name does not say interpreter, and it is the one to check: its three assertions are over `std.shell_stream_capture` predicates, and those predicates are deleted too, along with `bounded_shell_host_drain.rs` and the replay test. Genuine peer, whole vertical, no live guard vanished. **But the check found the same class one level up, where a test-function grain cannot see it.**"),
    p(text: "**A SURVIVING CARRIER STILL ADVERTISES A REPAIR THIS CUT DELETED.** `gunbc.roadmap_serve` `roadmap_serve_host_shell_bounded_drain_receipt_note` describes a bounded child-stderr drain as live, cites `bounded_shell_stream_capture_replay` BY NAME as its witness, and records the incident as NOT CLOSED. Measured at HEAD: `map_shell_outputs`, `CapturedStreamEvidence` and `ShellOutputLimitExceeded` each resolve to zero surviving files; the drain, the predicates and the named witness are all deleted. So a live module in a surviving lane asserts a mechanism that no longer exists, over an incident that is still open. **This is the vanishing-obligation class in its worst position — not a guard silently deleted, but a guard silently deleted while its receipt keeps reporting it in place.** Corrected at the note rather than deleted: deleting it would erase the incident along with the mechanism. **Declared rung lowering, per §4b(3):** the class was mechanically preventive (a bounded drain with a replay witness over a 59,522,411-byte stress specimen) and is now UNGUARDED, because the hazard belongs to any shell drain while the enforcement was interpreter-local. Restoration trigger unchanged and now the sole carrier of the obligation: the native shell transport must carry a bounded stderr tail and a typed stdout-overflow refusal before it drains a child. **CLASSIFIED PER ARM RATHER THAN PER ROW, because this obligation is disjunctive and I recorded only one arm when I wrote it.** Arm one — the native shell transport gains the bound — is an ADDITION, unthreatened by every cut in flight and dischargeable only by feature work. Arm two — the serve path that drains a child is itself deleted or never natively realized — is a DELETION, and it is reachable by a sibling lane rather than by me. **The arms have opposite consequences and I had stated the addition as if it were the whole trigger: under arm one the obligation is discharged and the hazard is closed; under arm two the obligation VANISHES WITH ITS SUBJECT and the hazard never existed for the successor.** A reader who checked only the arm I wrote would conclude this row can only ever be closed by building something, which is false, and would keep an obligation alive against a subject a sibling cut had already removed. **The generalisation for every lane deleting a vertical: your test-grain sweep will not find this. Grep the SURVIVORS for your deleted symbols and read what they claim about them.**"),
    p(text: "**THE TREE THIS CARRIER'S COUNTS ARE RELATIVE TO, stated once here rather than repeated per number.** Every measurement in this document was taken on `integration/interpreter-cut` — NEVER on `origin/main`, and never on a sibling integration branch except where a row names one explicitly. That matters because five integration branches are live and a count read without its tree is the misattributed receipt: a correct answer to a question the reader did not ask. **Stated at the honesty this carrier can actually support: the branch is uniform, the HEAD is not.** These rows were written across many commits, so a count taken early and a count taken late sit side by side under one branch name; where a head is load-bearing the row carries the sha (the sibling-branch measurement carries `635f5a17916394d01e7c1e520ad4ff0169fad35d`, the sub-grain sweep below carries `a5960bf983`), and where it does not, the row is branch-stated and head-unstated and should be re-measured rather than quoted. **The same decay governs positions, which is why this document cites symbols: a line number is tree-relative twice over — it moves under any edit above it, INCLUDING an edit made on a branch the reader is not on.**"),
    p(text: "**SUB-GRAIN SWEEP — THE CLASS A MODULE-GRAIN CHECK CANNOT SEE, RUN ON THIS CUT, AND IT COMES BACK CLEAN.** A registration whose MODULE is deleted is findable by any module-level sweep; a registration whose module is fine and whose DECLARATION was retired is invisible to it — and it is the likelier failure, because retiring a row is ordinary work while deleting a module is an event. This cut modified nine surviving `.dag` modules, and the declarations it removed from them are `emit_compiler_tests_module`, `is_lib_rs_macro_provider` and `order_lib_rs_mod_names`. **Zero surviving references to any of the three, and no roster registration names them.** The one further hit, `order_partial_lib_rs_mod_names`, is a prose mention inside this cut's own witness annotation — a dangling reference in documentation, already declared, not an enrollment surface. **INSTRUMENT CAVEAT, declared because it produced four false positives out of eight and would have produced findings if I had banked it: the extraction is LINE-grain, so a declaration whose TEXT I edited reads as removed-and-added and enters the candidate set as a deletion.** `generated_pub_mod_block`, `emit_lib_rs_from_files`, `artifact_extra_valid_note` and the `roadmap_serve` note are all alive and edited, not removed. Half the candidate list was an artifact of reading a diff at the wrong grain — in a sweep whose entire subject is reading at the wrong grain."),
    p(text: "**PART 3 OF THE THREE-PART RULE FOUND THE WORST DEFECT IN THIS CUT, AND IT IS IN `dag/std/`.** `std.primitive_identity` IMPORTS the module this cut deleted — `import gunbc.v1_interpreter_primitive_surface` naming `v1_interpreter_primitive_arms` and `v1_interpreter_row_count` — and it is not a stale citation but a live dependency: `primitive_interpreter_surface_rows` calls the deleted function and contributes one of FIVE authorities to the primitive-surface census. Gated against the oracle, the module answers with four located diagnostics: unresolved import, function not found in scope, a method-surface refusal cascading from it, and the row-count call. **A std-layer module is broken on this branch by this cut.** The blast radius is not std-local: `primitive_d0_derived_surface_authority_count` is the literal 5, `primitive_d0_interpreter_census_matches_roster` is a real guard joining census rows to the roster's own count, `primitive_d0_open_denominator_receipt_holds` conjoins them, and three of those symbols have consumers OUTSIDE std — `test.claim.primitive_identity_join_witness_test` and `gunbc.roadmap_authority`. **Not repaired here, deliberately.** The honest repair drops one authority from a census denominator, which is the empty-observation narrow §5 names unless the narrowing is declared, and it cascades into a witness and the roadmap authority — a multi-carrier change under a hold whose ruling may revert the whole cut. Stopped and declared, per the factory model: the line stops, analysis precedes restart. **The bearing on DESIGN is direct and already half-recorded: this is the `PrimitiveDefinition` denominator the determinism thread names as its dissolve-on, so deleting the interpreter narrows the forked authority set from five to four — which this cut already stated for the determinism fork and did NOT state here, because the carrier was never read.**"),
    p(text: "**TWO CLASSES OF DIAGNOSTIC ON THIS BRANCH ARE INHERITED RATHER THAN CAUSED, AND THEY ARE INVISIBLE TO DIFFERENT INSTRUMENTS — WHICH IS THE ONLY REASON BOTH WERE FOUND.** The first is the #8262 drift already recorded above: four `NonEmptyDiagnostics` mismatches that appear ONLY under a post-tightening binary, because this branch holds pre-tightening sources and a fork-base oracle cannot see a wall that landed after the fork. The second is the opposite shape. Three method-resolution refusals — `complement`, `meet`, `join` on `Container(BooleanAlgebra, Coproduct(Bool))` in `v2.test.claim.emit.logic_ground_truth_test` — were broadcast as arriving with the floor cut. **Measured here before integrating anything: they are already present, at this cut's fork base, under the PRE-#8262 oracle.** Both files involved (`logic_ground_truth_test.dag` and `std.algebra`) are byte-identical to `origin/main` and appear in no part of this cut's diff. **So they do not arrive with anything — they were already there, and what changes is only whether something LOOKS.** The distinction is worth keeping because the remedies differ: a compiler tightening creates new refusals of old code, while an unchecked path conceals old refusals of old code, and only the second is a §3 second-census concealment."),
    p(text: "**UPDATED WITHIN THE HOUR, AND THE UPDATE IS THE REASON THIS PARAGRAPH IS DANGEROUS IF LEFT AS WRITTEN: `logic_ground_truth_test.dag` HAS BEEN DELETED ON MAIN BY OPERATOR RULING.** So integrating main removes those three diagnostics from this branch rather than surfacing them, and **their absence afterwards is expected — it is NOT evidence that integration fixed anything, and NOT evidence this tree is wrong**, which are the two readings an absent expected error invites and both of which are false here. A prediction that fails silently is worse than no prediction, and a receipt asserting *these three are present* would have become one the moment the file left. **The measurement above still stands as a fact about THIS tree at THIS fork base, which is now the narrower and more useful claim.**"),
    p(text: "**AND BECAUSE THE FILE IS GONE FROM MAIN BUT STILL PRESENT HERE, THE REPRODUCTION IS CAPTURED WHILE IT IS STILL OBSERVABLE — the substrate defect is untouched by the deletion, and what the deletion removed was the only thing pointing at it.** The three call sites are one line each: `bool_boolean_algebra.complement(a)`, `.meet(a, b)`, `.join(a, b)`, over `v2.std.logic`'s `Bool`. The refusal is `method 'complement' cannot be resolved: receiver type 'Container(BooleanAlgebra, Coproduct(Bool))' establishes no method surface`. The measured root is a KEYING mismatch rather than a missing declaration: `std.algebra` registers the profile against the ELEMENT — the `(\"Bool\", BooleanAlgebraProfile)` row — while method-existence resolves through a lookup keyed on the CONTAINER name, so a container-keyed probe against an element-keyed registration misses by construction. **Reproduced here under a PRE-#8262 binary, which independently rules out the recent tightening as its cause and dates the defect at or before `64ebefa741`.** That last clause is the part worth keeping: with the only caller deleted, this receipt is one of the few remaining records that the class reproduces at all, and whoever brings the check back should not have to rediscover which of the two candidate causes it was."),
    p(text: "**THE INTEGRATION-GRAIN DISCRIMINATOR, RUN PREEMPTIVELY RATHER THAN AFTER A RED, WHICH IS THE WHOLE POINT.** The test — does the failing file appear in my own diff, and is it byte-identical to main — costs two commands and can be run BEFORE the errors show up, so the attribution exists before the temptation does. Run here it answers no and yes for both files, which settles those three permanently and means no future red on them needs re-litigating. **What it cannot do is answer the same question for a BEHAVIOURAL failure, where the file may be identical and the failure still real; there the discriminator is reproduction off CI.** And one asymmetry that must not be smoothed over when reading this branch's own red: a `build` failure here is deterministic and is not flake — nine resolution errors at four consecutive heads — so re-running it burns a runner and returns the same answer, whereas main's own redness lives entirely in `ci`. **Re-run advice belongs to `ci` and not to `build`, and reporting either at RUN grain rather than JOB grain is what makes them look like one number.**"),
    h2(text: "THE CENSUS — what exactly breaks when the interpreter goes"),
    p(text: "**THE NINE ARE AN ABORT POINT, SO THE CENSUS IS TAKEN AT THE SYMBOL GRAIN INSTEAD — WHICH THIS PARAGRAPH ONCE CALLED COMPLETE AND IS NOT, AS ITS OWN LAST CORRECTION SHOWS.** Resolution errors stop the target before type checking, so an error count measures where the reporter stopped; a reference count does not. Measured across the surviving seed: **559 references to 54 DISTINCT interpreter symbols. RE-MEASURED OVER THE WHOLE TREE WITH NO PATH OR TYPE FILTER — 565 references, 55 symbols — and the universe is stated because the first figure's was not.** The stage0-only scope missed six references and one symbol (`v1_interpreter::run`), spread across `src/v1/tests`, one `docs/plans` receipt, one `.dag` std module and this carrier. **AND THE RELATION ITSELF IS NARROW, WHICH IS A THIRD AND LARGER CORRECTION: 565 IS A LOWER BOUND, NOT A COUNT.** The census traverses exactly one edge — *text qualified by `v1_interpreter::`* — and the dependency class invisible to it is the ordinary one: a file that IMPORTS the names and then uses them BARE. Four files do that, and measured on just three symbols (`InterpContext`, `ExecutionMode`, `InterpResult`), the unqualified uses are **41 in `cli_run.rs`, 15 in the materialization consumer, 22 in the acceptance-history carrier, 4 in the placement bridge — 82 references the census could not see, from three of fifty-five symbols.** **`Value` WAS left unmeasured as ambiguous, and the ambiguity turned out to be decidable rather than inherent: NONE of the four files imports any other `Value`, so every bare occurrence in them resolves to the interpreter's.** Counted: 210 bare in `cli_run.rs`, 52 in the acceptance-history carrier, 15 in the materialization consumer, 15 in the placement bridge — **292 further references, taking `Value` alone from 222 to 514.** **So the census total moves from 565 to at least 939, a two-thirds increase, and `Value` by itself is now roughly 55 percent of every interpreter reference in the repository.** The honest residue: this disambiguation covers four symbols across four files, so fifty-one symbols and every other file remain measured only in the qualified relation, and a whole-word text match still cannot separate a use from a mention inside a comment. **The number is a better lower bound, not a count — and *at least 939* is the strongest form this instrument supports.** **The shape claim survives and in fact strengthens: six symbols still carry roughly 470 of 565, so the widened universe moved the total by one percent and moved the finding not at all.** That is worth recording precisely BECAUSE nothing changed — a re-run that confirms is as much a result as one that overturns, and a record where only the overturned checks get published reads as though narrow universes are usually fatal, which would make the next person skip the cheap ones. **The one hit that needed real checking was `dag/std/execution_mode.dag`, since a std module naming the interpreter is the shape that produced the below-floor break: read, it is a prose `String` note and the module imports only `std.types`. Not a dependency, and the difference between those two answers is one file read.** And the distribution is the finding, not the total — **six symbols carry 470 of the 559**: `Value` 222, `InterpContext` 107, `run_in_context_with_args` 50, `ExecutionMode` 46, `run_in_context` 31, `Symbol` 14. The remaining 48 symbols appear eight times or fewer, most of them exactly once. **So this is not 54 problems. It is two problems, four entry points, and forty-eight details** — and any plan that prices the interpreter cut by symbol count is pricing the tail."),
    p(text: "**THE TWO PROBLEMS ARE A VALUE REPRESENTATION AND AN EVALUATION CONTEXT, and everything else is downstream of them.** `Value` and `InterpContext` together are 329 of 559 references; they are what a native execution path must supply before any consumer can move, and no amount of work on the tail advances that. The four entry points — `run_in_context`, `run_in_context_with_args`, `ExecutionMode`, `Symbol` — are the shape of *resolve a `.dag` entry and evaluate it*, which is what `gunbc run` IS rather than how it happens to be built. **The forty-eight-symbol tail is almost entirely INSTRUMENTATION rather than semantics**: memo-frame counters, eval-recompute totals, fold and call-frequency snapshots, profiling clocks, output policy, verbosity, group syntax, trace grouping. That tail does not need porting — it needs deciding, once, whether terminal Y wants an equivalent instrument, and most of it retires with the evaluator it measured. **Stating the split matters because a 54-row worklist and a 2-row worklist recruit completely different plans, and the 54-row reading is the one an error census produces.**"),
    p(text: "**THE ROUTING NUMBERS THE SEQUENCING RESTS ON ARE A TOKEN GREP, AND AT ROW GRAIN ONE OF THEM IS OFF BY A FACTOR OF FIFTEEN — measured on files byte-identical to `origin/main`, so this is not a branch artifact.** The circulated figure is *NativeRouted 15, InterpretedRetained 20*. Counting `disposition:` ROWS instead of token occurrences: `witness_bulk_routing` carries 16 `InterpretedRetained` and **ZERO** `NativeRouted`; `native_routing_frontier` carries 3 `InterpretedRetained` and **ONE** `NativeRouted`; `emit_coverage_frontier` carries one `InterpreterRetained` and one `SelfEmittedNative`. **So the retained side is right — nineteen or twenty depending on whether the odd spelling counts — and the native side is ONE row, not fifteen.** The other fourteen `NativeRouted` occurrences sit inside `dissolution: unbound_dissolution(description: \"…\")` prose describing what the flip WOULD look like. **A count of a word that appears in the prose about a state is not a count of things in that state**, and the direction of the error is the dangerous one: 15-of-35 reads as a program 43 percent converted, while one row of twenty reads as a program that has barely started."),
    p(text: "**AND THE SPELLING IS FORKED, WHICH IS WHY A ROW-GRAIN COUNT NEEDED TWO PASSES: `InterpretedRetained` in two carriers, `InterpreterRetained` in a third.** One concept, two names, sibling files — §3's nicknaming class, and it is exactly the shape that makes a census miss by one no matter how carefully the counter greps, because the two spellings are one edit-distance apart and both read as correct English. Flagged rather than fixed: they are another lane's carriers, and the interpreter cut is not the place to consolidate a naming fork. **The census consequence stands on its own: any join over these dispositions that keys on the identifier will silently drop whichever spelling it did not think of.**"),
    p(text: "**THE ENTRY POINTS SPLIT BY CALLER, AND THE HOPEFUL ANSWER IS REFUSED: THE ROUTING SURFACE IS NOT SMALLER THAN FOUR. ELEVEN CALLERS, AND EXACTLY ONE IS DISPOSABLE.** The two `run_in_context` entry points are reached from eleven files. **Four are production consumers carrying 99 of the 121 references** — `cli_run.rs` at 56, `claim_executor.rs` at 30, the materialization consumer at 9, the placement bridge at 4 — and every one of those is a capability that survives terminal Y with a changed realization, not a site that disappears. **The other seven are probe, survey and measurement bins, which is precisely the population it would be convenient to call disposable — so it was CHECKED rather than assumed, and six of the seven are ENROLLED.** `auth_declared_but_unwired_witness` and `interp_recorded_fixture_witness` each carry a `dag/tools` transport and a `gunbc.ci_release_bins` registration; `frontier_probe_survey` has nineteen `.dag` references including its own witness test; `measure_dependency_view_build` and `wiring_liveness_whole_tree` sit in `gunbc.ci_layer_roots`; `realization_sweep_survey` backs a probe entry. **Exactly one caller — `v2_whole_tree_parse_scan`, one reference — has no `.dag` reference anywhere and is the only free deletion in the set.**"),
    p(text: "**THAT RESULT IS THE ONE WORTH HAVING PRECISELY BECAUSE IT IS THE UNWELCOME ONE.** The question was whether some entry points serve only consumers that are themselves deletion population, which would shrink the routing surface below four. Measured: they do not. Twenty-one of the twenty-two probe-side references belong to bins something enrolls, so calling them instrumentation and retiring them would delete enrolled consumers — the vanishing-obligation class, committed under the most sympathetic possible cover, which is *it was only a probe*. **This is the same carve-out the tail reading needed: `instrumentation` is a description of what a thing measures, never a licence to retire it, and the discriminator is enrolment rather than vocabulary.** The one bin that IS free is free because a join said so, not because its name sounded temporary — and its name sounds exactly as temporary as the six that are not."),
    p(text: "**TAIL INSPECTION DISCHARGED — THE MEMO COUNTERS ARE NOT FREE TO RETIRE, AND THE REASON IS NOT THE ONE THE CHECK WAS LOOKING FOR.** The commitment was to test whether any of the forty-eight is the only remaining way to observe a claim the repository still makes, before *instrumentation* became a category that retires unexamined. Measured across the eval-recompute and memo family — `take_process_eval_recompute_totals`, `eval_call_memo_frame_exit`, `eval_call_memo_counters`, `EvalRecomputeTotals`, `print_eval_recompute_trace`, `parse_memo_global_snapshot`, `TYPECHECK_COMPUTE_COUNT`: **every `.dag` reference to any of them is PROSE** — `String` notes and rendered paragraphs describing the mechanism — so no live claim consumes them and nothing dangles if they go. **That is the answer the check was built to find, and it is the wrong answer to act on.** Their Rust consumers are `claim_executor.rs`, `claim_batch.rs` and `cli_run.rs` — the floor itself, not the probe bins — and the capability they observe is MODELLED, at `extdeps.realization.eval_memo`, described there as the ladder's single-site discharge provider."),
    p(text: "**SO THE VERDICT INVERTS ON THE SECOND READING: THEY ARE THE OBSERVATION SURFACE OF A CLAIM THE REPOSITORY STILL MAKES AND CANNOT CURRENTLY CHECK.** The once-per-content-key law is live in `extdeps.realization.eval_memo` and in DESIGN; the warm-hit skip proof that executed against `TYPECHECK_COMPUTE_COUNT` is WITHDRAWN and owed, its own carrier reading `SkipProofWithdrawn`. **A withdrawn proof leaves no consumer behind, so a consumer-join over the corpus reports these counters as unreferenced — which is precisely the state a retired-but-unrestored claim produces, and it is indistinguishable by that join from genuinely dead instrumentation.** Retiring them would convert *the proof is withdrawn and restorable* into *the proof is withdrawn and the instrument it needs no longer exists*, at no visible cost on the day it happened. **The general form, which is the durable half: an enrolment join answers whether something is USED, never whether something is OWED, and for any claim whose evidence has already been withdrawn the two answers are opposites.** Recorded, nothing retired, and the restoration path is unchanged — re-establish the counter assertion as a floor witness, or retire the guard with the `Value::Null` split."),
    p(text: "**THE OWED-NOT-UNUSED DISCRIMINATOR, MEASURED — AND ITS PROPOSED TELL-SET IS MOSTLY FICTIONAL, WHICH WOULD REPRODUCE THE EXACT FALSE CLEAR IT EXISTS TO PREVENT.** The rule is right and the vocabulary offered for applying it is not: of the tells circulated — `SkipProofWithdrawn`, `NotDelivered`, `EvidenceUndecided`, a `Withdrawn` state, a not-yet-built marker, a named restoration trigger — only the first two name anything in this corpus, at three carriers each across eleven files total. **`EvidenceUndecided` and the rest match ZERO declarations anywhere.** A lane that adopts the rule and greps the full list gets nothing back for four of six terms, reads the empty result as *no withdrawn claim needs this*, and retires the subject — **a false clear produced by the discriminator's own vocabulary, in a rule whose entire subject is that an empty result can mean the opposite of dead.** The usable form is the two real tells plus the carriers they sit in, which is small enough to read rather than grep. **RE-MEASURED OVER THE WHOLE TREE WITH NO FILE FILTER, BECAUSE THE FIRST RUN OF THIS VERY CHECK USED A `.dag`-ONLY UNIVERSE: the counts move to 4 and 5, from 3 and 3.** The conclusion survives — the four fictional tells stay at zero unfiltered, so *names nothing* was a fact about the corpus and not about my filter — but the two real counts were narrow, and **the files my universe excluded are `DESIGN.md` and `docs/plans/five-minute-ci-gate-design.md`: the canonical design authority and a design note.** Half of that is redundant, since DESIGN.md is generated from a producer the `.dag` universe already contained; the docs/plans note is a genuine miss with no producer in scope. **A discriminator built to find OWED claims, run over a universe that excludes the documents where this repository writes down what it owes — and it was my own instrument, one message after I criticised someone else's tell-set for being incomplete.**"),
    p(text: "**SELF-APPLIED TO THIS CUT'S OWN DELETION SET, WHICH IS THE TEST THAT MATTERS SINCE THE RULE ARRIVED FROM MY OWN FINDING.** Joining every subject this cut deleted — the interpreter primitive surface and dispatch emit, `shell_stream_capture`, `bounded_shell_host_drain`, `recorded_fixture`, `coproduct_reflection`, `compiler_tests_rust` — against the eleven carriers that hold a real withdrawal tell: **only `v1_interpreter` itself appears, in `gunbc.roadmap_authority`, its witness, and `gunbc.design_document`.** Every other deleted subject is absent from every withdrawn-or-owed context, so the deletions stand under the discriminator rather than merely under the consumer join that preceded it. **The one hit is not a defect but the program's open question wearing its own name: whether the interpreter is dead or withdrawn is exactly what is being decided, and the discriminator correctly refuses to answer it from a corpus grep.**"),
    p(text: "**DID I CREATE THE ZERO — SELF-APPLIED, AND THE ANSWER IS YES ONCE, ON THE SUBJECT WHERE ALL THREE VARIANTS OF THE JOIN RULE NOW FIRE AT ONE TIME.** Checked against this cut's deletions: `extdeps.realization.hermetic_fixture` is NOT zeroed — six surviving `.dag` consumers including `std.cache_identity`, `std.cache_interface` and its own witness — so deleting `recorded_fixture.rs` removed a Rust realization, not the capability's consumers, and the earlier claim that the capability outlives is now measured rather than asserted. **`extdeps.realization.eval_memo` is a different answer: it now has ZERO Rust consumers, and this cut is what made that true**, because the eval-frame memo's realization lived inside the interpreter. **So the same capability is simultaneously reported dead by three independent mechanisms, each of a different kind:** its realization is gone because I deleted it (crisp-crab's variant — the zero is my footprint); its counters read unreferenced because the proof that consumed them is withdrawn (the variant from this lane); and its obligations live in prose and dissolution triggers a code-reference join cannot see (tidy-gull's). **Three zeros, three causes, one live capability with a live once-per-content-key law.**"),
    p(text: "**THAT CONVERGENCE IS THE ARGUMENT FOR RUNNING ALL THREE QUESTIONS RATHER THAN THE BEST ONE.** No single check would have cleared `eval_memo`: the enrolment join says unused, the withdrawal check finds a proof already retracted rather than a live consumer, and the footprint check is the only one that names the deletion as the cause — yet each of the three alone would have left a reader with a plausible *retire it* and no visible dissent. **The failure they share is that a zero has no provenance: the number does not record who made it, when, or whether anyone is owed it, and every mechanism that produces one produces the identical digit.** Nothing here retires; the entry is recorded so that whoever restores the skip proof knows the realization went with the interpreter and does not read its absence as evidence the memo was never real."),
    p(text: "**KEPT AS AN INSTRUMENT RATHER THAN A REPAIR LIST, which is this branch's remaining job.** The census is the only place this list exists, because it is only visible from a tree where the interpreter is actually absent — on any tree that still has it, every one of these 559 references resolves and nothing reports them. **That is the same shape as the rest of this document: the deletion is the census, and it can only be taken once from the far side.** What it says about sequencing is unambiguous: the twenty `InterpretedRetained` routing rows and the executor's own dependence on interpretation are not made smaller by repairing consumers, and no consumer-side work on this branch reduces the 329."),
    h2(text: "THE NINE — every fix-forward error classified, one by one"),
    p(text: "**The unhold's condition, discharged at the grain it asked for. Tree: `integration/interpreter-cut`, build job of run 31920674148, COMPLETED.** All nine live in `cli_run.rs` and its submodules. **Arm A — a capability Y should provide, FIVE.** (1) `cli_run.rs:10` imports `coproduct_reflection` `decl_facts_corpus_walk` and `DeclFactRaw`, used at three real call sites; the terminal reading of decl facts is a `Node`-tree walk and `v2.std.decl_facts_skeleton` is the standing candidate, its name a fair warning about completeness. (3) `cli_run/materialization_provider_consumer.rs` consumes `InterpContext`/`Value`/`ExecutionMode`; materialization is `std.materialize` keyed on `ContentHash` in the terminal model, so the consumer wants Y values, not interpreter ones. (4) `cli_run/test_module_hygiene_bridge.rs` is the `test fn` placement rule, whose dissolve-on DESIGN already names — placement moves to canonical source ingestion and test identities derive from parser-produced declarations — so this is arm A with its trigger written down before the cut existed. (5) `cli_run/roadmap_acceptance_history_carrier.rs` parses acceptance history out of `.dag` values; the history is a modeled fact and Y evaluates the carrier. (9) `cli_run.rs:40758` takes an interpreter `Value` to answer whether a qualified name resolves in a derived module set — that is `v2.compiler.resolve`'s question, asked through the wrong carrier."),
    p(text: "**Arm C — a need that retires with the interpreter, THREE, and each owes a receipt rather than a replacement.** (6) `cli_run.rs:17366` `emit_run_eval_profile` imports `cast_lookup_counters`, `eval_profile_snapshot`, `expr_variant_name` and `EXPR_VARIANT_COUNT` — profiling instrumentation for the interpreter's own evaluator, whose subject is gone; there is nothing to rebuild, only a receipt that the profile retired with the thing it profiled. (7) and (8) at `cli_run.rs:15615` and `:15630` are the same shape twice: `Option<Rc<recorded_fixture::RecordedFixtureStore>>` as a parameter of interpreter-context construction. The SITE retires with the context it built. **The hermetic-replay CAPABILITY behind it is a separate question and is NOT retired by this classification** — `extdeps.realization.hermetic_fixture` is where it would live — and conflating the two is how a capability disappears inside a mechanical cleanup. **Arm B — a form the terminal model rejects: ZERO. Arm D — another weed the error just named: ZERO.** And one error is not classifiable because it is not independent: (2) `cli_run.rs:31`'s bare `use crate::v1_interpreter;` is the aggregate import every site below draws on, so it resolves when the other eight do and names no work of its own. **Stated rather than folded into a neighbour, because a nine that silently becomes an eight is a count without a subject.**"),
    p(text: "**RECLASSIFIED — THE FIVE ARM-A ROWS ARE DELETION POPULATION, NOT MIGRATION SUBJECTS, AND NO Y REPRESENTATION IS OWED FOR ANY OF THEM.** The classification above was right and the instruction that followed it was not: *build it in Y* skips the question §3 puts first — will this be consumed substantially unchanged by terminal Y, or does a higher replacement root make it disappear? **The list's own shape answers it. Nine errors in one consumer is not nine loads; it is one consumer, and the consumer belongs to another root.** Verified here rather than relayed: every consumer of `decl_facts_corpus_walk`, `RecordedFixtureStore`, `qualified_name_resolves_in_derived_module_set` and `cast_lookup_counters` lives inside `src/v1/stage0` — with none outside it, at all — and that package is the frozen seed, scheduled for deletion whole. **So an *outside cli_run.rs* consumer is not evidence a capability survives; it is the same deletion envelope one file over.** Building five verticals to satisfy a consumer a sibling cut deletes is the §3 attractor in its documented form, and it recruited the classification's reader as readily as its author."),
    p(text: "**DELETION POPULATION DESCRIBES THE SITE, NEVER THE CAPABILITY — so each row says in one line whether something outlives the seed and where it would live. Named and homed, NOT built.** (1) decl-facts corpus walk: **capability outlives** — lens grounding, cited-symbol resolution and wiring liveness all want decl facts; home is the `Node`-tree read with `v2.std.decl_facts_skeleton` as the standing surface. (3) materialization provider consumption: **outlives** — home is `std.materialize` / the artifact-grain materialization kernel, keyed on `ContentHash`. (4) test-module placement: **outlives, and is the only one whose home DESIGN already fixed** — canonical source ingestion, with test identities derived from parser-produced declarations. (5) roadmap acceptance history: **outlives** — the history is a modeled fact; home is the acceptance carriers under `gunbc.roadmap_authority`. (9) qualified-name resolution: **outlives** — home is `v2.compiler.resolve` over the containment tree. And restating the two Arm-C rows under the same test: (7)(8) hermetic replay **outlives**, homed at `extdeps.realization.hermetic_fixture`; (6) eval profiling **does NOT** — it instrumented an evaluator that no longer exists, and if terminal Y wants profiling that is Y's own instrument rather than a migration of this one. **A capability with a name and a home survives a deletion; one that exists only as a call site does not, and the whole value of writing the line is that it forces the difference to be stated instead of assumed.**"),
    p(text: "**THE SEQUENCING ANSWER, STATED PLAINLY BECAUSE IT WAS ASKED PLAINLY: NO. THIS BRANCH CANNOT REACH GREEN FROM INSIDE THIS LANE, AND THAT IS A FACT ABOUT ORDER RATHER THAN A FAILURE OF THE CUT.** All nine remaining errors are in `cli_run.rs` or its submodules; none is in any file this cut owns. With the five reclassified as deletion population, the correct repair for them is not a bridge from here but the higher root — the `cli_run` / seed cut — and the three Arm-C rows retire with receipts that cost nothing and unblock nothing on their own. **So #8287 is not waiting on work someone forgot to do inside it; it is waiting on an ordering, and any green it could reach before that ordering would have to be manufactured by building representations terminal Y discards.** §3's fourth arm names this exactly: the error points at another weed, and the response is to back up to ITS root, never to build a bridge from yours. **The one thing this branch could still do alone was the below-floor `dag/std` break, and that is repaired and green.**"),
    p(text: "**Two constraints on this list that the list itself cannot show.** First, **every one of the nine sits in `cli_run.rs` or its submodules**, which is the region this cut was told not to expand into — so classification is mine and the edits are not, and acting on arms A and C means either that instruction is lifted or another lane executes them. Second, **the nine are an abort point, not a population**: E0432/E0433 stop the target before type checking, so this is the resolution wave only, and arms A and C will both grow once the lib links. **The classification is therefore complete over what is VISIBLE, and the honest form of that sentence is not *there are nine* but *nine are reachable from here*.** A near-miss worth recording beside it: (1) was first read as an UNUSED import and nearly filed as a free deletion, because the grep that looked for its uses was truncated to its first six hits and the three real call sites sit ten thousand lines further down. The instrument answered exactly what it was asked and the question was wrong — the same shape as every other quiet-instrument failure in this document, committed while classifying them."),
    p(text: "**THE PLACEMENT TEST HAS A SECOND EXIT, AND THIS CUT'S CROSS-CARRIER EDITS TAKE IT.** *Would this sentence still be true on a branch that never heard of my cut?* Applied to the three supersession clauses this cut wrote into carriers it does not own — `gunbc.roadmap_serve` and two in `gunbc.design_document` — the bare judgement fails: *the guard is gone* is simply false on a tree that still has it, and by the two-way test it belongs in a decision record rather than at the site. **But the clauses as actually written all name their own precondition — *deleted by the interpreter cut (2026-08-15)* — which makes them true on ANY branch: a reader without the cut reads them as a conditional about what that cut does, and a reader with it reads them as a completed fact.** So the NO branch of the test has two exits rather than one: **RELOCATE the claim, or SELF-LOCATE it by naming the precondition that makes it conditional.** The second is strictly better where it is available, because a supersession that lives only in a commit message is invisible to the carrier's next reader, and the carrier's next reader is exactly who the clause exists for. **Recorded with the honest provenance: this cut satisfies the test by a citation habit rather than by design — every supersession clause here dates and names the cut because §3 demands the citation, and the placement property fell out of that. A habit that happens to satisfy a rule is not the same as having applied it, and the difference matters for whether it holds next time.**"),
    p(text: "**MY OWN KNOWN-RED CONTROL DRAINED, AND I DRAINED IT MYSELF — the cross-lane hazard with the adversary turning out to be this lane.** The gate's three-way discrimination was demonstrated on `std.primitive_identity` as the known-RED subject; **this cut then REPAIRED that module, so the control now returns GREEN and has since the repair landed.** The second RED specimen, the `logic_ground_truth` method refusals, is deleted on main and drains the moment this branch integrates. **Two controls, two different adversaries — one a sibling lane's operator-ruled deletion, one me — and in neither case did anything announce that a control had lost its subject; the gate kept passing, because a control that stops discriminating still runs.** Stating it in the weak-but-true form rather than the strong-but-false one: those two were never independent of anything, they were CONVENIENT, and a convenient control is one whose subject you did not choose."),
    p(text: "**THE REPLACEMENT IS ADVERSARY-INDEPENDENT BY CONSTRUCTION RATHER THAN BY TOPIC, WHICH IS THE ONLY FORM THAT SURVIVES FIVE SIMULTANEOUS CUTS: a synthetic module held OUTSIDE the repository, in scratch, calling a function that exists in no lane's tree.** No cut can drain it because no cut can reach it; there is no dissolution row to read, because it has no row. **And the honest limit, measured rather than assumed: it lands on `shape1: parse walk panicked`, NOT on `shape3: located diagnostics` — so it controls the panic arm, and the diagnostic arm currently has NO adversary-independent control at all.** I had intended a shape3 control and reported what it actually produced. What would invalidate this one is a change in the compiler's own diagnostic behaviour — an ADDITION, not a deletion — so the accurate claim is *unthreatened by the cuts in flight*, never *independent*, and the standing obligation is to re-check it whenever the diagnostic surface changes rather than whenever a lane deletes something."),
    p(text: "**THE ORACLE'S OWN PROVENANCE, CHECKED RATHER THAN ASSERTED — AND THE BINARY I HAD BEEN USING WAS THE UNVERIFIED ONE.** The operator grant permits a main-built `gunbc` for compile and typecheck checks only, so every gate receipt in this document depends on the binary actually being main-vintage. **I had asserted that and never checked it.** Two binaries sit in scratch: `gunbc-oracle` (md5 `2be27b33…`, 17:14) and `gunbc-main` (md5 `8d538b21…`, 19:48, 2½ hours later). Only the first has provenance: it is byte-identical by md5 to `target/release/gunbc` inside a worktree checked out at `64ebefa74167cbcda034fed14702054e207b42e6` — this cut's fork base, verified as an ancestor of `origin/main`, with a clean status. `gunbc-main` matches nothing and its build tree is unknown. **Every gate I ran used `gunbc-main`, the one named as if it were main-built and the one that is not verified to be — a filename standing where a subject was owed, authored by me, for myself, in a document cataloguing that exact grain error.**"),
    p(text: "**THE TWO BINARIES WERE THEN RUN AGAINST EACH OTHER ON EVERY GATE THIS DOCUMENT CITES, AND THE FOURTH ONE DISAGREED — WHICH IS WORTH MORE THAN THE THREE AGREEMENTS.** Three entries answer IDENTICALLY under both: this carrier and `gunbc.design_document` both GREEN, `std.primitive_identity` both RED with the same four diagnostics at the same locations. The fourth, `gunbc.roadmap_serve`, splits: the fork-base binary answers GREEN, and the other answers RED with four `expected 'Product(NonEmptyDiagnostics)', got 'Coproduct(Diagnostics)'` mismatches in `v2.extdeps.languages.bash_command_fold` and `v2.workflow.bash_command_fold_serialize`. **Neither binary lied, and the disagreement identifies the unlabelled one rather than convicting it.** `origin/main` has advanced ten commits past this cut's fork base, and one of them is `aa1a3a8cd2` — *Refuse structured type mismatches at argument and field sites* (#8262) — which touches those exact two modules and introduces the `NonEmptyDiagnostics` carrier the diagnostic names. So the unlabelled binary is built from a main AT OR AFTER that tightening, and its diagnostic text is its own vintage fingerprint. **The two are not a good oracle and a suspect one; they are two oracles answering different questions — the compiler this branch was WRITTEN against, and the compiler a merge would JUDGE it by — and the receipts were never labelled with which.**"),
    p(text: "**A CONSEQUENCE THAT IS NOT ABOUT THE ORACLE AT ALL: THIS BRANCH CARRIES FOUR REAL DIAGNOSTICS UNDER CURRENT MAIN'S COMPILER, AND NONE OF THEM BELONG TO THIS CUT.** #8262 tightened the typechecker AND fixed the sources it newly refuses; this branch forked before both, so it holds the pre-tightening `bash_command_fold` sources while the newer compiler refuses them. That is ordinary ten-commits-behind drift, not a defect introduced here, and it is invisible to the fork-base oracle by construction. **It also retroactively explains a claim I made earlier and could not then justify:** when the `roadmap_serve` gate first came back red I reported those four as pre-existing and unrelated to my edit, reasoning that a `String` note edit cannot produce a type mismatch in another module. The conclusion was right and the argument was weaker than the conclusion — the actual reason is a compiler tightening I did not know had landed. **A correct answer reached by an argument that does not establish it is not a measurement; it is a guess that happened to agree with one, and it reads identically in a receipt.**"),
    p(text: "**THE RECEIPTS SURVIVE, BY EXECUTION RATHER THAN BY ARGUMENT.** Provenance is not the only way to establish an instrument; agreement with a provenance-verified peer is stronger, so both binaries were run on the same two inputs — one known-RED (`std.primitive_identity`, the below-floor break) and one known-GREEN (this carrier). **Byte-identical output on both: the same four diagnostics with the same locations, messages and ordering, and the same green with the same `resolved 24 sources`.** So the gate verdicts in this document stand as issued — **on the three entries where the two agree, and on the fourth as the FORK-BASE verdict specifically, which is the honest scope.** **AN ORACLE STATES ITS BUILD, and this one now does: `gunbc-oracle` is `64ebefa74167cbcda034fed14702054e207b42e6`, this cut's fork base, verified an ancestor of `origin/main` and md5-matched to a clean checkout — an ANCESTOR-VINTAGE oracle, which is not the same claim as *main* and must not compress into it, since that compression is precisely what produced the swapped names above. It is the right oracle for asking whether this cut's own edits parse and typecheck against the tree they were written for; it is the WRONG one for asking whether they would survive a merge, and this document makes only the first claim.** **The discriminating property was checked in the same pass, because a gate whose did-not-run output resembles its success output would make all of this moot: pointed at a nonexistent entry it answers `GATE RED [INFRASTRUCTURE: no verdict at all]`, at a broken module `GATE RED [shape3: 4 diagnostics]`, and at a clean one `GATE GREEN` with a source count — three distinguishable outputs, so silence is never available to be read as success.** That was built in deliberately, and it is the one instrument decision in this cut that needed no correction afterwards."),
    p(text: "**THIS CUT WROTE NO REPLACEMENT, AND THE MEASUREMENT SAYS SO: 30 LINES ADDED ACROSS ALL `.rs`, 26,640 REMOVED.** The carrier's own name invites the misreading — `interpreter_cut_replacement` is the successor PLAN, not code authored here — so it is worth pinning with numbers rather than assurance. Every one of those 30 added lines sits inside a GENERATED artifact being re-derived (two crate-layout rosters, the `lib.rs` module list, the emitter twin's concat); the only file created is this plan. **There is no reimplementation of the interpreter on this branch, in any language, at any fidelity.** That matters beyond bookkeeping: the replacement-work failure class — deleted code was correct, the replacement lost a property, nothing objected — cannot occur here, because there is no replacement to lose a property. The classes this cut CAN commit are the ones already recorded above: a deletion whose consumers survive, a modifier stranded onto a survivor, a receipt still advertising what left."),
    p(text: "**WHERE THE SIGNATURE-IS-A-CHECKLIST RULE DOES BIND, RUN TWO-SIDED AND CLEAN.** Exactly ONE signature changed in this entire cut: `emit_lib_rs_from_files`, whose second parameter was renamed `has_compiler_tests` to `has_hand_maintained_mods`. Checked at the CALL rather than the declaration, which is the half the rule exists to catch: base passes `(all_module_files: all_mod_files, has_compiler_tests: has_pipeline)`, head passes `(all_module_files: all_mod_files, has_hand_maintained_mods: has_pipeline)` — same arity, same argument, only the parameter name moved. Nothing a caller handed it was dropped. Every other `fn` removed by this cut left with its whole file. **But the rule's deeper form binds at a scale no signature check reaches, and it is this branch's central fact: `cli_run.rs` passes 462 references into the deleted module, and not one of them is visible from the deleted body — they are visible only from the call sites.** The two-sided checklist and the 462 are the same observation at different magnitudes: what a deletion costs is written where it was CONSUMED, not where it was defined."),
    p(text: "**THE EVIDENCE POSITION OF THIS BRANCH, STATED PLAINLY BECAUSE IT IS WORSE THAN ANY SINGLE FINDING HERE: NO CORPUS-DENOMINATED VERDICT HAS EVER EXISTED FOR IT.** Measured over all 25 workflow runs on `integration/interpreter-cut`, from the first at 2026-08-15T16:03Z to the latest at 2026-08-16T00:43Z: the `ci` job — the ~100-minute one carrying the compile-clean gate and the witness corpus — ran between SIX AND EIGHT SECONDS every single time and failed. It never did any work. Its failing step is the derived refusal that fires when an upstream job has already established the branch red, and `build` fails in about 45 seconds on the `cli_run.rs` wall. **So the compile-clean gate has never evaluated this tree, not once**, which is exactly why the `std.primitive_identity` break sat undetected: the per-entry oracle gates could not see it because it was in no closure I chose, and the corpus instrument that would have seen it has never run. **Two instruments, one narrow by construction and one never reached, and the gap between them is precisely where a below-floor regression in `dag/std/` was able to live.** A related reading is available and is NOT good news: this branch shows zero cancelled runs in a program where cancellations are 52 percent, and the reason is not discipline — nothing long enough to cancel has ever started. **A branch that cannot reach its verdict does not accumulate evidence; it accumulates untested pushes, and the absence of a red from an instrument that never ran is not the absence of a defect.**"),
    p(text: "**AND THE INSTRUMENT LIMIT THAT LET IT HIDE, which is mine and which invalidates the reassurance rather than the receipts.** Every oracle gate run on this branch was per ENTRY, and an entry gate resolves one import closure — the plan-carrier gate reports `resolved 24 sources` against a corpus of 3,703 `.dag` files. Those greens are true and narrow. I have been reading them as evidence about the corpus, which they cannot be: `std.primitive_identity` is in no closure I gated, so no green I hold ever looked at it. **A per-entry gate's silence about a file it never opened is not a clean bill; it is the fail-fast lower bound one layer out, at the closure grain rather than the diagnostic grain.** The correction is not more gates on entries I choose — that repeats the sampling — it is that a corpus claim requires a corpus-denominated run, which on this branch is the compile-clean gate and nothing I can do by hand."),
    p(text: "**THE SURVIVOR GREP RUN PROPERLY, AND IT IS A POPULATION RATHER THAN AN ANECDOTE.** Grepping every surviving `.dag`, `.rs` and `.toml` for this cut's deleted module names returns: `recorded_fixture` 19 files, `coproduct_reflection` 14, `v1_interpreter_dispatch_generated` 7, `ci_control_plane` 6, `data_initializer_identity` 6, `v1_compiler_compiler_tests_rust` 1. All six source files are confirmed deleted here. Many references will be legitimate — a frontier row this cut itself corrected, a v2 module whose v1 twin died, a receipt recording the deletion — and the triage is per reference, at the same grain the test rule uses: not *does it name a deleted symbol* but *is what it claims still true*. **What is owed to the lanes that own those carriers is the population, not my classification of it**, and it is reported rather than swept, because the sweep is the move that turns a live claim into a silent one."),
    p(text: "**THE ONE I CAN ALREADY CLASSIFY IS MINE, AND I GOT IT WRONG AT THE MODULE GRAIN.** `ci_control_plane` was deleted here as a dead bin — correctly at the MECHANISM grain: `ci_control_plane.rs` imported `v1_interpreter` `InterpContext` and `Value` and evaluated `.dag` through the interpreter, so it is a genuine peer and could not have been repaired in place. **Its ROLE is live and has readers.** `gunbc.owned_ci_seed` carries `owned_ci_control_plane_seed_scaffold`, a REGISTERED `Scaffold` with a bind and a dissolution condition, rooted in `gunbc.doc_graph_roots`; `gunbc.owned_ci_serve` and `gunbc.owned_ci_page` both describe themselves as reading run state that `ci_control_plane` WRITES. So the cut deleted the writer of a live read path and left the readers, the scaffold row and the plan root standing. **Four-arm verdict: mechanism GONE, role NOT succeeded — and the arm that matters is the one with no successor.** Not restorable in place, since the mechanism was an interpreter evaluation; the obligation is that owned CI v0's writer is now unimplemented, and the carriers still say otherwise. Declared here rather than repaired in five other lanes' modules under a hold. **The lesson generalises past this cut: a bin can be dead at every grain a compiler can see and alive at the only grain that matters.**"),
    p(text: "**THE LEDGER ENTRY THAT DOES NOT NEED THE GRANT — every row classified by reading the producer beside the artifact, three states.** `derived, unconfirmed` was the honest label while regeneration was the only imagined test; it is not the only test, and the cheaper one costs one file read per row and lands findings the grant question was holding hostage. **STRUCTURALLY CONSISTENT — the two crate-layout artifacts.** `gunbc.stage0_crate_layout_emit` emits both by literal concatenation, and the `.rs` half carries an `rustfmt::skip` attribute, so no formatting pass sits between producer and artifact and byte-grain reading is legitimate. Both halves read one input, `hand_maintained_stage0_filenames`, so the artifacts must agree by construction — and the join EXECUTES: 20 names in the `.dag` list, 20 in the `.rs` slice, symmetric difference empty. That is a real check with a real failure mode, because it catches one half regenerated against a stale other. **NOT COMPARABLE BY READING — `gunbc_stage0_crate_layout_generated.rs`.** Its producer is the whole v1 emitter over a `.dag` module, not a literal concat, so a single-file read cannot decide its bytes. What CAN be joined is the data it carries against the `.dag` it was compiled from, and that join agrees. Reported as its own state rather than folded into either neighbour: the answerable question and the asked question are different, and saying so is the whole discipline."),
    p(text: "**KNOWN TO DIFFER — `lib.rs`, on two counts, and the read found a defect the drift ledger had no row for.** The first divergence is NOT one: the producer emits its `allow` attribute on a single line and the committed artifact carries it expanded across many, which is rustfmt and not drift — excluded deliberately, because a reader who counts formatting as divergence will stop reading before reaching the real one. **The apparent second one — order — is not a divergence either, and it was CLAIMED HERE AS ONE BEFORE THE TOOL WAS RUN.** The first version of this row read: the committed block is alphabetical, `generated_pub_mod_block` is not, and rustfmt does not reorder module items, therefore drift. The middle clause is false. rustfmt sorts module declarations by default, which the formatter demonstrated within one minute of the commit attempt by sorting them. **The correction is worth more than the finding it replaced, because it is the same defect this program keeps naming, committed by its own author: a property of an instrument asserted from reading rather than established by running it, and asserted in the direction that made a row look substantive.** The attribute below was additionally acting as a REORDERING BARRIER — rustfmt does not sort across it — which is why the block sat unsorted at the fork base and sorted itself the instant the attribute left. So the order question is fully explained by the formatting pass and the artifact carries no order divergence at all. **The one real divergence is mine, and it is a defect this cut produced: a stranded attribute.** At the fork base a `macro_use` attribute sat on `v1_interpreter_dispatch_generated`, the first entry of the block. This cut deleted that entry. The attribute was on its own line and so survived the deletion, re-attaching to whatever became first — `cli_run`, which declares no macros. **This is the guard-that-left-with-its-subject class inverted: a deletion can also strand a modifier onto an unrelated survivor.** It is provably inert — zero `macro_rules` declarations remain anywhere in the stage0 sources, so the attribute exports nothing and its removal cannot change compilation — which is exactly why no build, no test and no drift gate would ever have reported it. Removed. **The general shape, offered because every lane holding an emitted twin can run it tonight: read your producer and your artifact side by side and classify. A formatting pass makes byte-reading illegitimate; an `rustfmt::skip` makes it legitimate; a compiled producer makes it impossible but usually leaves a DATA-grain join that is not. And do not ASSERT what your formatter does or does not touch — RUN IT, because that assertion is exactly where a false divergence and a missed one both come from.**"),
    p(text: "**Net-unchanged check: RUN, measured zero.** No file in this cut's diff has zero added and zero removed, so the remove-and-restore shape — which passes every diff-based check by construction — has no instances here. Recorded as a checked zero rather than an absent row."),
    p(text: "**Artifact:** `DESIGN.md`. **Producer:** `gunbc.design_document`. **Revisions behind:** three, all in this branch — (1) the numeric guard's SUPERSEDED clause, (2) the content-hash R0 backstop's SUPERSEDED clause with its bounded rung drop, (3) the `std.determinism` fork denominator narrowed-not-closed, (4) §3's cite-the-symbol example repointed from `v1_interpreter.rs` `observe_tool_identity` to `std_content_hash.rs` `content_hash_family`. **Producer is correct; the artifact is stale.** The stale sentences are still in the committed `.md`."),
    p(text: "**Why it is not regenerated here, which is a property of this class of branch rather than a lapse: a cut that removes the seed cannot regenerate the artifacts whose producers it is obliged to correct — the obligation and the capability leave in the same commit.** Regeneration runs a `gunbc` built from the seed this cut deletes. The main-vintage oracle would emit correct bytes from the corrected producer, but the operator grant on it is compile and typecheck ONLY, and regenerating a committed artifact is an execution that lands bytes; reading a typecheck grant as a generation grant is the improvise-past-doubt move this repository names. **Ruled a landing step, performed with a working binary by whoever merges.** DESIGN does not forbid the mechanism — its own list of permitted roles for a deleted X includes a bootstrap producer from history or a pinned ref — so what is missing is a grant, not a principle. **And the artifact is TERMINAL, which is what makes declaring sufficient:** nothing depends on `DESIGN.md` compiling, so staleness costs currency and not capability. A generated artifact UPSTREAM of the build would not have that luxury — there both sides must move together or the tree is dead, and this cut has none in that position, because the one it had (`v1_interpreter_dispatch_generated.rs`) was deleted together with its producer and its roster."),
    h2(text: "ORPHANED BY THIS CUT — two enrolled transports, declared at identity grain"),
    p(text: "**CORRECTED BY MEASUREMENT, 2026-08-16 — the sentence that opened this section was false, and false in the self-exculpating direction.** It read: this one is mine in a way the scaffold index is not, nobody orphaned that file, its subject died from several directions at once. Measured: `gunbc.language_source_scaffold_index` carries 58 `carrier_module` rows, and **41 of them cite `v1.compiler.compiler_tests_rust` — the module THIS cut deleted.** Not several directions; one, and it is mine. A further row cites `v1.compiler.emit_rust`, which this cut modifies and v1-cut deletes. The disposition (a follow-on, not a widening of this cut) is unchanged; the ATTRIBUTION was wrong, and it was wrong because the earlier reading grepped for the index's own state rather than joining its rows against this cut's deletion set. **The check that found it is the declared-symbol grain: the carrier name goes to zero because you deleted it, which tells you nothing — grep what the carrier DECLARED, and join the survivors against it.** I orphaned these too, by deleting three bijection tests and leaving two.** Surviving: `tools.interpreter_dispatch_bijection_real_roster_transport` and `test.claim.interpreter_dispatch_bijection_real_roster_witness_test`. Their stated subject is a roster↔handler bijection proven by PERTURBING `gunbc.v1_interpreter_primitive_surface` or `src/v1/stage0/src/v1_interpreter.rs` and requiring a real cargo refusal. This cut deletes both perturbation targets AND the Rust integration target the transport invokes (`--test interpreter_dispatch_bijection_real_roster_red`), so the check can no longer fail for the reason it was written."),
    p(text: "**Six enrollments, one of them a roadmap acceptance gate:** `gunbc.ci_layer_roots` (falsifier substrate pattern), `v2.test.claim.witness_admission_test`, `v2.test.claim.witness_exclusion_reconciliation_test`, `v2.test.claim.self_host.wet_receipt_enrollment_witness_test`, `v2.compiler.self_host.wet_receipt_enrollment` — which states that **R1 roadmap acceptance is gated on a falsifier receipt naming this exact witness identity** — and `gunbc.cargo_execution_placement` with its witness. Retiring them from inside this cut would touch v2 test carriers and a roadmap gate, which is a widening of the cut's boundary; RULED a follow-on. The declaration is the part that is owed here."),
    p(text: "**RUNG ON THE ANSWER BELOW, stated before the answer so it cannot be rounded up: READ, NOT EXECUTED.** No witness runs on this branch at all — there is no `claim_batch` binary, because the crate does not build — so the verdict is derived from the arms' own text and the types they return, tracing `holds` back to its single `true` arm and that arm back to `run.success`. Strong evidence; not an observation. What would settle it is an execution nobody on this branch can perform, and that is the same blocker the generated-artifact drift has."),
    p(text: "**ASKED AND ANSWERED, because refuse and pass have opposite consequences: THEY REFUSE.** `run_interpreter_dispatch_bijection_real_roster_red` = `cargo_outcome_holds(run_..._outcome())`, and `gunbc.cargo_execution_placement` `cargo_outcome_holds` returns `true` on `CargoInvocationPassed` ALONE — `CargoInvocationFailed` and `CargoInvocationNotAttempted` both answer `false`. `CargoInvocationPassed` is reachable only from `run.success`, and the shell it runs invokes `cargo test --test interpreter_dispatch_bijection_real_roster_red` on a test target this cut deleted, which exits nonzero. So the orphan lands on `CargoInvocationFailed { exit_code, cause }` — **loud, typed, and carrying its cause**, not a green on nothing. **This is therefore ordinary residue and NOT a fail-open oracle on a roadmap gate**, which was the consequential alternative. The typed outcome is what makes the answer readable at all: its own header records that the predecessor returned a bare `run.success`, under which an absent cargo, a failed clone, and a compile that wrongly SUCCEEDED were indistinguishable for ten days."),
    h2(text: "Shared seam with the cli_run cut — ruled, not a blocker"),
    p(text: "56 of the 105 errors are `cli_run.rs` (48) plus `materialization_provider_consumer` (5), `test_module_hygiene_bridge` (3), `roadmap_acceptance_history_carrier` (3). Roughly 38 of the interpreter's 125 host builtins were implemented inside `cli_run.rs`. **THE CLAIM THAT FOLLOWED FROM THAT WAS FALSE AND IS RETRACTED, 2026-08-16.** This row read: `cli_run.rs`, which the sibling lane is deleting on its own branch, so the builtin bridge dies from both sides and no host feed survives on either. Measured here rather than relayed, at that branch's head `635f5a17916394d01e7c1e520ad4ff0169fad35d`: `cli_run.rs` is PRESENT at 42,077 lines, `v1_interpreter.rs` is PRESENT at 14,910, and its deletions are `.dag` carriers and probe bins — no engine file at all. **NO OPEN WORK DELETES `cli_run.rs`.** So the bridge does not die from both sides; it dies from one, and this branch is stranded against a 42k-line engine cut that nobody has started. **The defect in how I came to believe otherwise is the one this program keeps finding, and I inherited it rather than measuring around it: the sibling branch's TITLE says it deletes the engine, and a title is not a subject** — the same grain error as a filename, a carrier name, or a compiler error's location. The consequence for the operator decision is larger than for this row: DELETE on the interpreter does not unblock one PR, it requires an engine cut that does not exist, and the sibling lane is a downstream CONSUMER of that ruling rather than a co-atomic party. This cut does not expand into `cli_run.rs`; bridge errors are triaged and reported, never rebuilt. The per-lens question (each census feed's lens either is already dead per DESIGN §6's 2026-08-11 deletions, or migrates to reading the `Node` tree) is DEMAND-DEFERRED: it becomes live only when each lens re-establishes on the native execution path."),
  ]
}

data interpreter_cut_replacement_plan: Plan = Plan {
  slug: "interpreter-cut-replacement",
  title: "Plan — interpreter cut replacement (delete-first)",
  body: interpreter_cut_replacement_body(),
  retirement: PlanRetiresWhen { condition: unbound_dissolution(description: "Every census class is backed out or retired with a receipt and the workspace links without an interpreter: the eval-entry quartet answered by emitted-and-compiled typed calls, the eval-profiling and CLI-output-policy retirements discharged, and the cli_run builtin-bridge seam closed from both sides. At that point the seed carries no dynamic Value universal and this plan is redundant.")
  }
}
